 ## Subtopics
 
-### Value-based Edge Estimation
-Finds edge location by interpolating to the mean of neighboring samples. It is robust to lower-order filters like linear interpolation and does not suffer from derivative discontinuities [S1, unofficial]. See [Value-based Edge Estimation](link).
+### Aggregate Importance Sampling
+- **What it is**: A method that combines importance sampling with aggregation techniques to improve the efficiency of gradient estimation.
+- **Difference**: Focuses on sampling strategy rather than derivative computation.
+- **When to choose**: When computational cost of sampling is a bottleneck.
+- **Link**: [Aggregate Importance Sampling](6471-aggregate-importance-sampling.md)
 
-### Hessian Eigenvector Analysis
-Uses the eigenvectors of the Hessian to determine edge orientation. See [Hessian Estimation via Convolution](6469-hessian-estimation-via-convolution.md).
+### Second Directional Derivative
+- **What it is**: Estimates the derivative along a specific direction rather than the full Hessian.
+- **Difference**: Simpler than full Hessian estimation but less comprehensive.
+- **When to choose**: When only directional information is needed.
+- **Link**: [Second Directional Derivative](11718-second-directional-derivative.md)
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Accuracy**: Low for sampled data with common reconstruction filters (linear, quadratic, cubic-spline) due to discontinuities in derivatives [S1, unofficial].
-- **Complexity**: Depends on the cost of computing the Hessian, which is $O(N)$ for $N$ samples if using finite differences or convolution kernels.
-- **Strengths**: Theoretically sound for continuous, unsampled data where the PSF is known and smooth [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations**: Unreliable for discrete sampled data; requires a reconstruction filter with a continuous second-order derivative, which is rare in standard graphics pipelines [S1, unofficial].
-- **When to use**: Only when working with continuous data or when a high-order, smooth reconstruction filter with continuous second derivatives is available and necessary.
-- **When not to use**: For standard linear or cubic-spline interpolated data in volume rendering, as it produces ambiguous or inaccurate results [S1, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity**: Depends on the cost of evaluating the second derivative and the root-finding algorithm.
+- **Memory Complexity**: Low, as it operates on the reconstructed field values.
+- **Strengths**: Provides a theoretically sound method for edge location in continuous data.
+- **Limitations**: Highly sensitive to the continuity of the reconstruction filter. Fails for common low-order interpolation schemes (linear, quadratic, cubic-spline) [S1, unofficial].
+- **When to use**: When a high-order reconstruction filter with continuous second derivatives is available and accurate edge localization is critical.
+- **When not to use**: When using standard linear or quadratic interpolation, or when computational resources are limited and a simpler value-based method suffices [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-This method originates from signal processing and medical imaging literature, specifically addressing the "partial volume effect" where edges are blurred [S1, unofficial]. It was proposed as a way to locate step edges accurately between samples by leveraging the properties of the point-spread function [S1, unofficial]. The limitations in sampled data were highlighted in studies comparing derivative-based and value-based edge detection methods [S1, unofficial].
+This method originates from signal processing and volume visualization research, specifically in the context of accurate iso-surface rendering and edge detection in medical applications [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the partial volume effect, where edges are blurred in sampled data, by leveraging the properties of the point-spread function [S1, unofficial].
 
